WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.437
A cleaned value should be explainable.

00:00:02.437 --> 00:00:07.892
In these examples, we remove formatting noise and apply a documented abbreviation rule.

00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:10.552
We are not inventing new information.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:18.472
Keeping raw and cleaned values side by side is useful during development because it makes every transformation

00:00:18.472 --> 00:00:23.182
visible. That helps catch over-cleaning and makes the process easier to audit.

00:00:23.182 --> 00:00:28.607
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:28.607 --> 00:00:34.187
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:42.097
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:42.097 --> 00:00:48.002
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.002 --> 00:00:54.074
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
